EXAMPLES = {
 # ---- Electronics ----
 'Что такое закон Ома?': ("What is Ohm's law?", "Ohm's law: **U = I·R** — the voltage across a part of a circuit equals the current times the resistance. Hence I = U/R and R = U/I.\n\nExample: a 220 Ω resistor on 5 V → I = 5/220 ≈ 22.7 mA, power P = U·I ≈ 0.11 W.\n\nYou can calculate it right in chat: \"U=5 R=220\"."),
 'Что такое законы Кирхгофа?': ("What are Kirchhoff's laws?", "**Current law (KCL):** the currents flowing into a node equal the currents flowing out: ΣI = 0. Charge does not pile up in a node.\n\n**Voltage law (KVL):** around any closed loop the sum of EMFs equals the sum of voltage drops: ΣE = Σ(I·R).\n\nHow to use them: 1) mark the branch currents (any direction — a negative result means the current flows the other way); 2) write KCL for (nodes − 1) nodes; 3) write KVL for the independent loops; 4) solve the system.\n\nTry the \"Kirchhoff: two sources and a load\" calculator in the Electronics skill."),
 'Что такое метод узловых потенциалов?': ("What is nodal analysis?", "Nodal analysis: take one node as ground (0 V); the potentials of the other nodes are the unknowns. Write Kirchhoff's current law for every node, expressing currents through potentials: I = (φ1 − φ2)/R. You need as many equations as nodes minus one — usually fewer than with mesh analysis.\n\nFor one node with branches (E1, R1), (E2, R2) and a load R3: U = (E1/R1 + E2/R2)/(1/R1 + 1/R2 + 1/R3)."),
 'Что такое метод контурных токов?': ("What is mesh analysis?", "Mesh analysis: give every independent loop its own \"mesh\" current and write Kirchhoff's voltage law for it: the loop's own resistance × its current − the shared resistances × the neighbouring mesh currents = the sum of EMFs in the loop. The current in a shared branch is the difference of the mesh currents. Handy when there are fewer loops than nodes."),
 'Что такое теорема Тевенина?': ("What is Thévenin's theorem?", "Thévenin's theorem: any linear circuit, seen from two terminals, can be replaced by a voltage source Uth with a series resistance Rth.\n\n- Uth is the open-circuit voltage at the terminals;\n- Rth is the resistance between the terminals with voltage sources shorted and current sources opened (or Uoc/Isc).\n\nExample: a 10 kΩ / 10 kΩ divider from 12 V is a 6 V source with 5 kΩ inside. With a 5 kΩ load the output drops to 3 V."),
 'Что такое теорема Нортона?': ("What is Norton's theorem?", "Norton's theorem: a linear circuit seen from two terminals can be replaced by a current source In (the short-circuit current of the terminals) with a parallel resistance Rn (the same as Thévenin's). They convert into each other with Uth = In·Rn."),
 'Что такое метод наложения?': ("What is the superposition theorem?", "Superposition: in a linear circuit with several sources, the current in any branch is the sum of the currents caused by each source alone. The other sources are replaced meanwhile: a voltage source by a wire, a current source by an open circuit. It does not work for power — power is not linear."),
 'Как работает делитель напряжения?': ("How does a voltage divider work?", "Two resistors in series between Vin and ground, the output is the middle point: **Vout = Vin·R2/(R1 + R2)** (R2 is the bottom one).\n\nExample: 12 V, R1 = 10 kΩ, R2 = 4.7 kΩ → 3.84 V.\n\nImportant: a divider is not a power supply. Its output resistance is R1‖R2, so the load must be 10+ times larger than R2 or the voltage sags. Use a regulator to power things. Calculate: \"divider 12V 10k 4.7k\"."),
 'Что такое конденсатор?': ("What is a capacitor?", "A capacitor is two plates separated by a dielectric; it stores charge Q = C·U and energy W = C·U²/2. The current through it is I = C·dU/dt: it blocks DC (once charged) and passes AC more easily the higher the frequency (Xc = 1/(2πfC)).\n\nTypes: ceramic (small values, decoupling, RF), electrolytic (large values, polarised, have ESR), film (precise, audio and mains filters), tantalum. Choose by capacitance, voltage rating with a 1.5–2× margin, temperature and ESR."),
 'Что такое катушка индуктивности?': ("What is an inductor?", "An inductor stores energy in its magnetic field: W = L·I²/2. The voltage across it is U = L·dI/dt, so the current through it cannot change instantly: breaking the circuit suddenly causes a voltage spike (that is why relays and motors get a diode across them). Its AC resistance XL = 2πfL grows with frequency. Used in switching converters, filters, LC tanks and chokes."),
 'Что такое реактивное сопротивление?': ("What is reactance?", "Reactance is the \"resistance\" of capacitors and inductors to AC, without losing energy (it is stored and given back).\n\n- Capacitor: Xc = 1/(2π·f·C), the current leads the voltage by 90°.\n- Inductor: XL = 2π·f·L, the current lags by 90°.\n\nThe impedance of an RLC circuit: Z = √(R² + (XL − Xc)²)."),
 'Что такое резонанс?': ("What is resonance?", "Resonance in an LC circuit is the frequency where XL = Xc: f0 = 1/(2π√(LC)). A series circuit has minimum impedance at resonance (just R) and maximum current; a parallel one has maximum impedance. The Q factor tells how sharp the peak is. Used in filters, oscillators, radios and wireless charging."),
 'Как работает диод?': ("How does a diode work?", "A diode conducts in one direction — from anode to cathode (the band on the body). It opens at about 0.6–0.7 V forward voltage (silicon), 0.2–0.4 V (Schottky), 1.8–3.3 V (LEDs). In reverse almost no current flows until breakdown.\n\nUses: rectifiers, reverse-polarity protection, flyback diodes on relays and motors, logic."),
 'Что такое стабилитрон?': ("What is a Zener diode?", "A Zener diode works reverse-biased: at its Zener voltage Uz it starts to conduct and keeps the voltage almost constant. It needs a series resistor: R = (Uin − Uz)/(Iload + Iz.min). Good for simple references and protecting inputs; to power a load, a regulator is more efficient."),
 'Как работает биполярный транзистор?': ("How does a bipolar transistor work?", "A bipolar transistor (NPN/PNP) is controlled by base current: Ic = β·Ib (β is 50…300). The base–emitter junction opens at ~0.65 V.\n\nAs a switch (NPN, load in the collector, emitter to ground): Rb = (Uctrl − 0.65)/Ib, with Ib 5–10 times larger than Ic/β so the transistor saturates, Uce ≈ 0.1–0.3 V.\n\nExample: 3.3 V from an MCU, 100 mA load, β = 100 → Ib ≈ 5 mA → Rb ≈ 530 Ω, take 470 Ω."),
 'Как работает MOSFET?': ("How does a MOSFET work?", "A MOSFET is controlled by the gate–source voltage Ugs; almost no current flows into the gate (only to charge its capacitance). When on, it is a resistance Rds(on), losses P = I²·Rds(on).\n\nAs a switch: an N-channel between the load and ground (low-side). For 3.3/5 V from an MCU use a logic-level MOSFET (Rds(on) specified at Ugs = 2.5–4.5 V, e.g. IRLZ44N, AO3400). Add 10–100 Ω in series with the gate and 10–100 kΩ gate-to-source so it stays off while the MCU resets."),
 'Чем MOSFET отличается от биполярного транзистора?': ("MOSFET or bipolar transistor — what's the difference?", "A bipolar transistor is driven by base current, a MOSFET by gate voltage.\n\n- MOSFET: almost no drive current, low losses at high current (Rds(on) is milliohms), fast; but it needs enough Ugs and a driver at high frequencies.\n- Bipolar: opens at 0.65 V, cheap and simple for small currents, but has a 0.1–0.3 V saturation drop and needs base current.\n\nAbove ~0.5 A a logic-level MOSFET is usually better."),
 'Как управлять нагрузкой от микроконтроллера?': ("How do I switch a load from a microcontroller?", "An MCU pin gives 5–20 mA, so loads are switched through a transistor:\n\n1. **N-MOSFET (logic-level)** between the load and ground: gate through 100 Ω to the pin, 10 kΩ gate-to-source to ground.\n2. **NPN transistor** for small currents: base through 1 kΩ.\n3. For inductive loads (relay, motor, solenoid) add a flyback diode across the load (cathode to +).\n4. Join the grounds of the MCU and the power side; for 230 V mains use only ready relay modules or solid-state relays with galvanic isolation."),
 'Зачем диод параллельно реле?': ("Why is there a diode across a relay?", "A relay coil is an inductor. When it is switched off, its current cannot vanish instantly, and a spike of tens to hundreds of volts appears on the switch and kills the transistor. A flyback diode (cathode to +supply, anode to the switch) lets that current circulate through the coil instead. 1N4148 or 1N4007 will do. If fast release matters, use a diode + Zener or a TVS."),
 'Что такое операционный усилитель?': ("What is an op-amp?", "An operational amplifier amplifies the difference of two voltages with a very large gain. With negative feedback it keeps U+ = U−, and resistors set the gain:\n\n- inverting: K = −Rf/Rin;\n- non-inverting: K = 1 + Rf/Rin;\n- follower: K = 1 (buffer).\n\nLook at the supply (rail-to-rail or not), the bandwidth (GBW) and the offset. Calculator: \"Op-amp gain\"."),
 'Что такое компаратор?': ("What is a comparator?", "A comparator compares two voltages and outputs a logic 1 or 0. An op-amp without feedback can do it, but a dedicated comparator (LM393, LM339) is faster and often has an open-collector output — it needs a pull-up. To stop the output from chattering near the threshold, add hysteresis: positive feedback with a resistor from the output to the + input."),
 'Как работает таймер 555?': ("How does a 555 timer work?", "The NE555 compares the capacitor voltage with 1/3 and 2/3 of the supply. As an astable oscillator, C charges through R1+R2 and discharges through R2:\n\n- f = 1.44/((R1 + 2·R2)·C);\n- t_high = 0.693·(R1+R2)·C, t_low = 0.693·R2·C.\n\nExample: R1 = 10 kΩ, R2 = 100 kΩ, C = 10 µF → ≈ 0.69 Hz. Calculate: \"555 10k 100k 10uF\"."),
 'Чем линейный стабилизатор отличается от импульсного?': ("Linear or switching regulator — what's the difference?", "A linear regulator (LDO, 7805, AMS1117) burns the extra voltage as heat: P = (Uin − Uout)·I. Simple and quiet, but efficiency = Uout/Uin.\n\nA switching regulator (buck steps down, boost steps up) switches current through an inductor, 80–95% efficient, but adds ripple and needs careful layout.\n\nRule of thumb: if voltage difference × current exceeds ~0.5–1 W, use a switcher."),
 'Что такое RC-фильтр?': ("What is an RC filter?", "An RC low-pass filter: a resistor in series, a capacitor to ground. It passes frequencies below fc = 1/(2πRC) and attenuates higher ones by 20 dB/decade. Swap R and C to get a high-pass filter.\n\nExample: 10 kΩ and 100 nF → fc ≈ 159 Hz. Calculate: \"rc 10k 100nF\"."),
 'Как прочитать цветовую маркировку резистора?': ("How do I read resistor colour bands?", "Start from the end where the bands are closer together. 4 bands: two digits, multiplier, tolerance. 5 bands: three digits, multiplier, tolerance.\n\nDigits: black 0, brown 1, red 2, orange 3, yellow 4, green 5, blue 6, violet 7, grey 8, white 9. Multiplier: the same digit as a power of ten, gold ×0.1, silver ×0.01. Tolerance: gold ±5%, brown ±1%.\n\nExample: yellow-violet-red-gold = 47·10² = 4.7 kΩ ±5%. Type the colours in chat, or \"colour code 4.7k\"."),
 'Что такое ряд E24?': ("What is the E24 series?", "E-series (E6, E12, E24, E96) are the standard values of resistors and capacitors. E12: 1.0 1.2 1.5 1.8 2.2 2.7 3.3 3.9 4.7 5.6 6.8 8.2 (×10ⁿ), ±10%. E24 adds 1.1 1.3 1.6 2.0 2.4 3.0 3.6 4.3 5.1 6.2 7.5 9.1, ±5%. E96 is for ±1% resistors. Round a computed value to the nearest one in the series."),
 'Как выбрать мощность резистора?': ("How do I choose a resistor's power rating?", "Compute P = I²·R = U²/R and take a resistor rated at least twice that: 0.125 W (0805), 0.25 W (1206 and the usual through-hole), 0.5 W, 1 W, 2 W. A resistor working at its limit runs hot and drifts."),
 'Как защитить схему от переполюсовки?': ("How do I protect a circuit from reverse polarity?", "Options:\n\n1. A series diode — simple, but loses 0.3–0.7 V (less with Schottky).\n2. A P-MOSFET in the positive line (drain to the input, source to the circuit, gate to ground through a resistor) — almost lossless.\n3. A diode across the input + a fuse — reversed polarity blows the fuse.\n4. A connector that physically cannot go in the wrong way."),
 'Что такое ESR конденсатора?': ("What is a capacitor's ESR?", "ESR is the equivalent series resistance of a capacitor. Ripple current loses power in it, P = I²·ESR, so the capacitor heats up and filters worse. It matters for electrolytics in switching supplies: a dried-out electrolytic has a high ESR (a common fault). Ceramic and polymer capacitors have a low ESR."),
 'Как работает Н-мост?': ("How does an H-bridge work?", "An H-bridge is four switches around a load (usually a motor). Turning on the diagonal pairs reverses the current and the rotation; PWM on the switches sets the speed. Never turn on both switches of one side — that is shoot-through, so drivers add dead time. Ready chips: L298N (old, lossy), TB6612FNG, DRV8833, BTS7960."),
 'Как работает трансформатор?': ("How does a transformer work?", "A transformer is two windings on a common core. AC in the primary creates a changing magnetic flux that induces an EMF in the secondary: U1/U2 = N1/N2, the currents the other way round: I1/I2 = N2/N1. It does not transform DC. Used to change voltage and for galvanic isolation."),
 'Что такое переменный ток?': ("What is alternating current?", "Alternating current changes direction periodically; mains is a 50 Hz sine (60 Hz in the Americas). The RMS value U = Upeak/√2: 230 V at the socket is about 325 V peak. Power with a reactive load: P = U·I·cos φ."),
 'Опасно ли напряжение 220 В?': ("Is 230 V mains dangerous?", "Yes. Mains voltage can kill: about 30 mA through the body is already dangerous. Rules: work only on a de-energised circuit, check with a tester that there is no voltage, don't touch a power supply board right after switching off (capacitors stay charged), use an RCD, ready modules with galvanic isolation and an enclosure. Without experience, let an electrician do the mains part."),
 'Как измерить ток мультиметром?': ("How do I measure current with a multimeter?", "Current is measured in series: break the circuit and put the meter in the gap. Move the probe to the A or mA socket and pick a range with margin. Never connect the meter in current mode across a source — that is a short circuit (the fuse or the meter dies). Voltage is measured in parallel, resistance only in an unpowered circuit."),
 'Что такое земля и общий провод?': ("What are ground and the common wire?", "The common wire (GND) is the point all voltages are measured from. All parts of a circuit that exchange signals must share a ground, otherwise the signal has no return path. Protective earth (PE) is a separate wire for the safety of the enclosure. On a PCB use a solid ground plane and short return paths."),
 'Что такое KiCad?': ("What is KiCad?", "KiCad is free electronics design software. The workflow:\n\n1. **Schematic** (Eeschema): symbols, wires, labels, annotation, ERC check.\n2. Assign footprints.\n3. **PCB** editor: Update PCB from Schematic, placement, routing, ground pours, DRC check.\n4. Gerbers and drill files for the fab, BOM, 3D view.\n\nAttach a .kicad_sch or .kicad_pcb file with the paperclip — I'll list the parts and nets and check the schematic."),
 'Что такое SimulIDE?': ("What is SimulIDE?", "SimulIDE is a simple real-time circuit simulator: resistors, capacitors, transistors, logic, an oscilloscope and a voltmeter, plus AVR, PIC and Arduino microcontrollers running your firmware (.hex). Great for learning and quick checks. Circuits are .sim1 files (XML) — attach one in chat and I'll list the parts and connections and help find the mistake."),
 'Чем симуляция отличается от реальной схемы?': ("How does a simulation differ from a real circuit?", "A simulator uses idealised models: no parasitic capacitance or inductance of traces, no interference, no component tolerances, heating, contact bounce or soldering quality. So a circuit that works in SimulIDE or LTspice may be noisy, oscillate or overheat on a breadboard. Check your calculations against datasheets, keep margins on power and voltage, and measure with an oscilloscope."),
 'Что такое ERC и DRC?': ("What are ERC and DRC?", "ERC (Electrical Rules Check) checks the schematic: unconnected pins, outputs tied together, power nets without a source (KiCad uses a PWR_FLAG for that). DRC (Design Rules Check) checks the board: clearances, track widths, unrouted nets, overlaps. Run both before ordering boards."),
 'Что такое netlist?': ("What is a netlist?", "A netlist describes a circuit without the drawing: which parts there are (R1 10k, U1 ATmega328) and which of their pins are joined in each net. KiCad uses it to carry the connections to the board, and simulators build their model from it. Attach a netlist or a schematic file and I'll check the connections net by net."),
 'Как читать электрическую схему?': ("How do I read a schematic?", "1. Find the supply and ground (VCC, +5V, GND) and follow where they go.\n2. Split the schematic into blocks: power, MCU, inputs, outputs, interfaces.\n3. Labels with the same name are connected even without a drawn wire.\n4. A dot at a crossing is a connection; without a dot the wires just cross.\n5. Designators: R resistor, C capacitor, L inductor, D diode, Q transistor, U integrated circuit.\n6. Check IC pins against the datasheet."),
 # ---- Physics ----
 'Что такое законы Ньютона?': ("What are Newton's laws?", "1. **First (inertia):** a body keeps its velocity (or rest) unless forces act on it or they cancel out.\n2. **Second:** acceleration is proportional to the net force and inversely proportional to mass: **F = m·a**.\n3. **Third:** two bodies act on each other with equal and opposite forces: F12 = −F21 (applied to different bodies, so they don't cancel)."),
 'Что такое второй закон Ньютона?': ("What is Newton's second law?", "Newton's second law: **F = m·a** — the net force equals mass times acceleration. To accelerate a 2 kg body at 3 m/s² you need 6 N. In general F = Δp/Δt — force is the rate of change of momentum."),
 'Чем вес отличается от массы?': ("What is the difference between weight and mass?", "Mass (kg) measures a body's inertia and is the same everywhere. Weight (N) is the force with which a body presses on a support or stretches a hanger; at rest on Earth P = m·g ≈ 9.81·m. In a lift accelerating upwards the weight grows, in free fall it is zero, while the mass does not change."),
 'Что такое импульс?': ("What is momentum?", "Momentum p = m·v (kg·m/s) is a vector. Conservation: in a closed system the total momentum is constant. The change of momentum equals the impulse of the force: F·Δt = Δp — an airbag lengthens the impact time and so reduces the force."),
 'Что такое закон сохранения энергии?': ("What is the conservation of energy?", "Energy is neither created nor destroyed, only converted from one form to another. In mechanics without friction Ek + Ep = const: a body falling from height h reaches the speed v = √(2gh). With friction part of the mechanical energy becomes heat."),
 'Что такое работа и мощность?': ("What are work and power?", "Work A = F·s·cos α (J) is the energy a force transfers over a displacement s. Power N = A/t (W) is how fast work is done; at constant speed N = F·v. 1 kWh = 3.6 MJ."),
 'Что такое закон Архимеда?': ("What is Archimedes' principle?", "A body immersed in a liquid or gas feels an upward force equal to the weight of the displaced fluid: Fa = ρ·g·V. A body floats if its average density is lower than the fluid's."),
 'Что такое давление?': ("What is pressure?", "Pressure p = F/S (Pa) is force per unit area. In a liquid p = ρ·g·h grows with depth (10 m of water ≈ 1 atm ≈ 100 kPa). Pascal's law: pressure on a fluid is transmitted equally in all directions — that is how a hydraulic press works."),
 'Что такое удельная теплоёмкость?': ("What is specific heat?", "Specific heat c is how much heat warms 1 kg of a substance by 1 °C: Q = c·m·ΔT. Water 4200 J/(kg·°C), aluminium ≈ 900, copper ≈ 385. Heating 1 l of water from 20 to 100 °C takes 4200·1·80 = 336 kJ — about 3 minutes for a 2 kW kettle."),
 'Что такое первый закон термодинамики?': ("What is the first law of thermodynamics?", "Q = ΔU + A: the heat given to a gas goes into changing its internal energy and into the work the gas does. In an isothermal process ΔU = 0, in an adiabatic one Q = 0, at constant volume A = 0."),
 'Что такое закон Кулона?': ("What is Coulomb's law?", "Two point charges interact with the force F = k·|q1·q2|/r², k ≈ 8.99·10⁹ N·m²/C². Like charges repel, unlike ones attract. The force falls with the square of the distance: twice as far — four times weaker."),
 'Что такое электрическое поле?': ("What is an electric field?", "The electric field is how charges act on each other. Its strength E = F/q (V/m). For a point charge E = k·q/r², between capacitor plates E = U/d. Voltage is the work the field does moving a unit charge."),
 'Что такое магнитное поле?': ("What is a magnetic field?", "A magnetic field is created by moving charges (currents) and magnets and is described by the flux density B (T). A current-carrying wire feels the Ampère force F = B·I·L·sin α, a moving charge the Lorentz force F = q·v·B·sin α. The direction follows the left-hand rule."),
 'Что такое электромагнитная индукция?': ("What is electromagnetic induction?", "When the magnetic flux through a loop changes, an EMF appears in it: ε = −ΔΦ/Δt (Faraday's law). The minus is Lenz's law: the induced current opposes the change of flux. Generators, transformers, induction hobs and ignition coils work this way."),
 'Что такое закон Джоуля–Ленца?': ("What is Joule's law?", "Current through a conductor releases heat Q = I²·R·t. That is why thin wires heat up at high current, and power lines use high voltage — for the same power the current is lower and the I²R losses drop."),
 'Что такое колебания?': ("What are oscillations?", "Oscillations are repeated motion around an equilibrium. The period T is the time of one cycle, the frequency f = 1/T. Pendulum: T = 2π√(L/g), mass on a spring: T = 2π√(m/k), LC circuit: T = 2π√(LC). At resonance an external force at the natural frequency sharply increases the amplitude."),
 'Что такое волна?': ("What is a wave?", "A wave carries oscillations and energy through space without carrying matter. Speed v = λ·f. Longitudinal (sound) and transverse (light, waves on a string). Sound in air ≈ 340 m/s, light 300,000 km/s."),
 'Что такое преломление света?': ("What is refraction of light?", "At the boundary of two media light changes direction: n1·sin α = n2·sin β (Snell's law), where n = c/v is the refractive index (water 1.33, glass ≈ 1.5). Going into an optically less dense medium at a large angle gives total internal reflection — optical fibre relies on it."),
 'Как работает линза?': ("How does a lens work?", "A converging lens brings parallel rays to the focus F. The thin lens formula: 1/F = 1/d + 1/f (d — to the object, f — to the image). Optical power D = 1/F in dioptres. Magnification = f/d."),
 'Что такое фотоэффект?': ("What is the photoelectric effect?", "The photoelectric effect is light knocking electrons out of a material. A photon's energy is E = h·f, and an electron escapes only if it exceeds the work function: h·f = A + m·v²/2 (Einstein's equation). Intensity sets the number of electrons, frequency their energy. Photodiodes and solar cells work this way."),
 'Что значит E=mc²?': ("What does E=mc² mean?", "Mass and energy are equivalent: E = m·c², c = 3·10⁸ m/s. Even a small mass is a huge energy: 1 g ≈ 9·10¹³ J. The mass defect in nuclear reactions explains the energy of nuclear power plants and of the Sun."),
 'Что такое ускорение свободного падения?': ("What is the acceleration of free fall?", "The acceleration of bodies falling without air resistance: g ≈ 9.81 m/s² at the Earth's surface (a bit more at the poles, less at the equator). On the Moon ≈ 1.62 m/s². The fall time from height h: t = √(2h/g), the speed v = √(2gh)."),
 'Что такое КПД?': ("What is efficiency?", "Efficiency η = useful work / work spent · 100%. It is always below 100% — part of the energy turns into heat. An electric motor 80–95%, a car engine 25–40%, an LED about 30–50% into light."),
 # ---- Programming ----
 'Что такое указатель?': ("What is a pointer?", "A pointer is a variable that holds the address of another variable. `int x = 5; int *p = &x;` — p points to x, `*p` reads or changes x. nullptr points nowhere. Dangers: dereferencing nullptr or a dangling pointer (to freed memory) is undefined behaviour. In C++ use std::unique_ptr and std::shared_ptr instead of raw owning pointers."),
 'Чем ссылка отличается от указателя?': ("How is a reference different from a pointer?", "A reference (`int &r = x;`) is another name for an existing object: always initialised, never null, never rebound. A pointer can be nullptr, can be redirected and supports arithmetic. For function parameters that cannot be empty prefer references (const & for reading)."),
 'Что такое стек и куча?': ("What are the stack and the heap?", "The stack holds local variables and function calls: allocated and freed automatically, very fast, but limited (usually 1–8 MB, a few KB on an MCU). The heap is dynamic memory (new/malloc): large, and you decide the lifetime. Typical bugs: stack overflow (deep recursion, big arrays), heap leaks and fragmentation."),
 'Что такое RAII?': ("What is RAII?", "RAII (Resource Acquisition Is Initialization): a resource is acquired in an object's constructor and released in its destructor. Memory, files and mutexes are then released automatically when the scope ends, even on an exception. Examples: std::unique_ptr, std::lock_guard, std::fstream."),
 'Что такое умные указатели?': ("What are smart pointers?", "std::unique_ptr — the only owner, frees the object in its destructor, can only be moved (std::move). std::shared_ptr — shared ownership with a reference count. std::weak_ptr — watches a shared_ptr without owning it, breaks cycles. Create them with std::make_unique / std::make_shared."),
 'Как найти причину segmentation fault?': ("How do I find the cause of a segmentation fault?", "1. Build with debug info: `-g -O0`.\n2. Run under gdb: `gdb ./app`, `run`, after the crash `bt` — the call stack shows the line.\n3. Enable AddressSanitizer: `-fsanitize=address,undefined` — it reports out-of-bounds, use-after-free and so on with the exact line.\n4. Or valgrind: `valgrind ./app`.\nCommon causes: nullptr, array out of bounds, a dangling pointer, stack overflow."),
 'Чем процесс отличается от потока?': ("How is a process different from a thread?", "A process is a separate program with its own memory; processes are isolated and talk through pipes, sockets or D-Bus. Threads are several flows of execution inside one process sharing memory: fast to exchange data, but they need synchronisation (mutexes, atomics) or you get data races."),
 'Что такое мьютекс?': ("What is a mutex?", "A mutex is a lock only one thread can hold at a time — it protects shared data from simultaneous writes. In C++: `std::mutex m; { std::lock_guard lock(m); ... }` — the lock is released automatically. Danger: deadlock when two threads take two mutexes in different orders."),
 'Что такое git rebase?': ("What is git rebase?", "`git rebase main` replays your branch's commits on top of the latest main — the history becomes linear. `git merge` instead creates a merge commit and does not rewrite history. Rule: don't rebase commits that are already pushed and used by others."),
 'Как отменить последний коммит?': ("How do I undo the last commit?", "If it is not pushed: `git reset --soft HEAD~1` — the commit is undone, the changes stay staged. `git reset --hard HEAD~1` also throws the changes away (careful!). If it is pushed: `git revert HEAD` — a new commit that undoes the changes without rewriting history."),
 'Что такое сложность алгоритма?': ("What is algorithmic complexity?", "Big-O shows how time (or memory) grows with the input size n. O(1) constant, O(log n) binary search, O(n) one pass, O(n log n) a good sort, O(n²) nested loops. For 1,000,000 items O(n²) is 10¹² operations, O(n log n) about 2·10⁷."),
 'Что такое сигналы и слоты в Qt?': ("What are signals and slots in Qt?", "Signals and slots are Qt's event mechanism: an object emits a signal (`emit valueChanged(v)`) and the connected slots are called. Connect with `connect(sender, &Sender::valueChanged, receiver, &Receiver::onValue);`. Across threads the call is queued to the receiver's thread automatically. The class needs Q_OBJECT."),
 'Как работает CMake?': ("How does CMake work?", "CMake generates build files (Ninja, Make) from CMakeLists.txt. Minimal:\n```cmake\ncmake_minimum_required(VERSION 3.21)\nproject(app CXX)\nadd_executable(app main.cpp)\n```\nBuild: `cmake -S . -B build -G Ninja` and `cmake --build build`. Libraries come in with find_package and target_link_libraries."),
 'Что такое неопределённое поведение?': ("What is undefined behaviour?", "Undefined behaviour (UB) in C/C++ is an operation the standard gives no meaning: out-of-bounds access, signed int overflow, dereferencing nullptr, reading an uninitialised variable, a data race. The program may \"work\", crash, or misbehave only with optimisation. Catch UB with -fsanitize=undefined,address and -Wall -Wextra."),
 # ---- Embedded ----
 'Что такое подтягивающий резистор?': ("What is a pull-up resistor?", "A pull-up resistor connects an input to the supply so that with the button open the input reads a clean logic 1 instead of \"floating\" and picking up noise. The button then goes between the input and ground (pressed = 0). Typically 4.7–10 kΩ; most MCUs have built-in ones (~20–50 kΩ), e.g. INPUT_PULLUP in Arduino or GPIO_PULLUP in STM32 HAL. A pull-down is the same to ground."),
 'Чем отличается SPI от I2C?': ("SPI or I2C — what's the difference?", "I2C: two lines (SDA, SCL), device addresses, open drain with pull-ups (usually 4.7 kΩ), 100 kHz / 400 kHz / 1 MHz, half duplex; handy for many slow sensors. SPI: four lines (SCK, MOSI, MISO and a separate CS per device), push-pull outputs, tens of MHz, full duplex; better for displays, memory, ADCs. I2C saves pins, SPI is faster and easier to debug."),
 'Что такое прерывание?': ("What is an interrupt?", "An interrupt is a signal from a peripheral (timer, pin, UART, ADC…) that makes the CPU pause the main program, run a handler (ISR) and return. Rules: keep the handler short (set a flag, put a byte into a buffer), no delays or printf; variables shared with the main program are volatile, multi-byte ones are read in a critical section; don't forget to clear the interrupt flag."),
 'Зачем нужен развязывающий конденсатор?': ("Why do I need a decoupling capacitor?", "A decoupling capacitor, usually a 100 nF ceramic, sits as close as possible to every supply pin of a chip. It supplies the short current spikes of switching and absorbs supply noise; without it the MCU may glitch or reset. Often 1–10 µF is added per board or group of chips."),
 'Как рассчитать резистор для светодиода?': ("How do I calculate an LED resistor?", "R = (Usupply − U_LED) / I. For example, a 3.3 V supply, a red LED ~2.0 V, 10 mA: R = (3.3 − 2.0) / 0.01 = 130 Ω → take 130–150 Ω. Power P = I² × R ≈ 0.013 W. Make sure the current does not exceed what the MCU pin allows (often 8–20 mA)."),
 'Что такое ШИМ?': ("What is PWM?", "PWM is a square wave of constant frequency with a variable duty cycle (share of time at 1). The average voltage = Usupply × duty cycle. Used for LED brightness, motor speed, servos, a DAC through an RC filter. An MCU timer generates it: the ARR register sets the period (STM32), CCR the duty cycle."),
 'Что такое DMA?': ("What is DMA?", "DMA (direct memory access) moves data between peripherals and memory without the CPU: an ADC buffer, UART reception or output to an SPI display. The CPU only sets up the channel (source, destination, length) and gets an interrupt when it is done or half done."),
 'Зачем volatile в C для микроконтроллеров?': ("Why use volatile in C on microcontrollers?", "volatile tells the compiler that a value can change outside the current code (in an interrupt handler, in a peripheral register, in another RTOS task), so it must not be cached in a register or have \"redundant\" reads removed. Without volatile, `while(!flag)` can become an endless loop after optimisation. volatile does not make access atomic — use critical sections for that."),
 'Что такое watchdog?': ("What is a watchdog?", "A watchdog timer resets the MCU if the program does not \"feed\" it in time. It recovers from hangs: feed it in the main loop only when everything works, not in a timer interrupt. STM32 has the IWDG (independent, with its own oscillator) and the WWDG (window)."),
 'Что такое UART?': ("What is UART?", "UART is an asynchronous serial interface: TX and RX lines (crossed) and a common ground, no clock, so both sides use the same speed (e.g. 115200 baud) and format 8N1. MCU levels are 3.3 or 5 V; RS-232 needs a converter (MAX3232), a PC a USB-UART adapter."),
 'Как работает АЦП?': ("How does an ADC work?", "An ADC turns a voltage into a number: code = Uin / Uref × (2ⁿ − 1). A 12-bit ADC with a 3.3 V reference has a step of ≈ 0.8 mV, 1.65 V → ~2048. Mind: don't exceed the reference, consider the source impedance and the sampling time, add an RC filter and average. A voltage divider lets you measure higher voltages."),
 'Как управлять мотором с микроконтроллера?': ("How do I drive a motor from a microcontroller?", "Not straight from a pin — you need a switch. For one direction: a logic-level N-MOSFET (e.g. IRLZ44N, AO3400) on the low side, 100 Ω into the gate, 10–100 kΩ gate-to-source and a flyback diode across the motor. For reversing — an H-bridge (DRV8833, TB6612). Speed — PWM. A separate motor supply with the grounds joined."),
 "Qu'est-ce qu'une résistance de pull-up ?": ("Qu'est-ce qu'une résistance de pull-up ?", "A pull-up resistor (résistance de pull-up) connects an input to the supply so that with no signal the input reads logic 1 instead of floating. Typically 4.7–10 kΩ; the button goes between the input and ground (masse, GND)."),
 'Что такое RTOS?': ("What is an RTOS?", "An RTOS (real-time operating system, e.g. FreeRTOS or Zephyr) splits a program into prioritised tasks and switches them with a scheduler with predictable latency. Tasks talk through queues, semaphores and mutexes. Useful when many independent things happen; simple devices do fine with a super-loop and interrupts."),
 'Как рассчитать частоту таймера STM32?': ("How do I calculate an STM32 timer frequency?", "Update frequency: **f = fclk/((PSC + 1)·(ARR + 1))**. Example: a 72 MHz timer, 1 kHz wanted → (PSC+1)(ARR+1) = 72,000 → PSC = 71, ARR = 999 (1 µs tick). For PWM the duty is set by CCR: D = CCR/(ARR+1). The \"MCU timer: PSC and ARR\" calculator finds the values for you."),
 'Как рассчитать скорость UART?': ("How do I calculate a UART baud divider?", "STM32 (oversampling 16): BRR = fclk/baud, e.g. 72 MHz / 115200 = 625 (0x271). AVR: UBRR = fclk/(16·baud) − 1; at 16 MHz and 115200 the error is −3.5% — enable U2X (UBRR = 16, error +2.1%) or use a 14.7456 MHz crystal. Up to ±2% error is acceptable. Calculate: \"uart 72MHz 115200\"."),
 'Что такое дребезг контактов?': ("What is contact bounce?", "When pressed, a mechanical button closes and opens many times for a few milliseconds — the MCU sees several presses. Fix it in software (treat the state as stable if it hasn't changed for 10–20 ms) or in hardware (an RC network of 10 kΩ + 1 µF and a Schmitt-trigger input)."),
 'Что такое I2C адрес?': ("What is an I2C address?", "Every device on an I2C bus has a 7-bit address (e.g. 0x3C for an SSD1306 OLED, 0x68 for an MPU6050). In the first byte of a transfer the address is shifted left by 1 and the low bit is read/write: 0x3C → 0x78 (write), 0x79 (read). That is why datasheets show both forms. An I2C scanner finds the addresses."),
 'Как уменьшить потребление микроконтроллера?': ("How do I reduce a microcontroller's power consumption?", "1. Sleep modes (STOP/STANDBY, deep sleep) and waking on an interrupt or RTC.\n2. A lower clock.\n3. Gate the clocks of unused peripherals.\n4. Unused pins as analog inputs or with a pull, not floating.\n5. Remove an LDO with a high quiescent current and the power LED.\n6. Compute the average current from the share of time spent active."),
 # ---- Other skills ----
 'Давай поговорим': ("Let's talk", "Sure. What's on your mind right now?"),
 'Что такое игровой цикл?': ("What is a game loop?", "A game loop reads input, updates the state of the world and draws a frame, one after another. The loop repeats for as long as the game runs."),
 'Что такое стоицизм?': ("What is Stoicism?", "Stoicism teaches telling apart what depends on us and what doesn't. It suggests focusing on our own judgements and actions."),
 'Как написать хорошее письмо?': ("How do I write a good email?", "Start with the purpose, add the context that's needed and end with a concrete next step. Short paragraphs and a clear subject help the reader grasp the request quickly."),
}

